% =======================================================
\section{Engineering Adaptation Pipeline}
\label{sec:adaptation}
\label{sec:adapt-impl}

The preceding section explains why the wave time scale, acceleration level, and
integral-state regularization are the natural quantities to move with sea state.
This section describes the estimator that supplies those quantities and the
smoothing and cadence rules used by the deployed implementation.  The design is
intentionally exogenous to the MEKF: no displacement, velocity, covariance, or
MEKF attitude state is fed back into the tuner.

\subsection{Measurement-only vertical acceleration}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}

The wave statistics require a levelled vertical acceleration, but levelling it
with the MEKF attitude would make the schedule a function of the state it is
controlling.  The implementation therefore uses a private Mahony complementary
observer \cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary} driven only by raw gyro and
accelerometer measurements,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct\varepsilon&=\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}\times\hat{\vct d}(\hat q),
\qquad
\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}=-\vct f^{B}/\lVert\vct f^{B}\rVert,\\
\dot{\hat q}&=\tfrac12\,\hat q\otimes
\bigl(\vct\omega^{B}+\vct\beta+k_P\vct\varepsilon\bigr),
\qquad
\dot{\vct\beta}=k_I\vct\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The resulting up-positive acceleration is
\begin{equation}
a^{P}_{\uparrow,k}
=-\!\left(\left[\mat{R}_{NB}(\hat q_k)\vct f^{B}_k\right]_{D}+g\right).
\label{eq:proxy-vertical}
\end{equation}
Only the proxy tilt is used; its yaw is unobservable and irrelevant to the
vertical channel.  The default gains are $k_P=\SI{0.1}{\per\second}$ and
$k_I=\SI{0.01}{\per\second\squared}$ (the source stores $2k_P,2k_I$).  The
correction corner is deliberately below the wave band so that gyro propagation
carries wave-frequency attitude while the accelerometer trims slow tilt and the
integral term removes standing gyro-bias tilt.  The same observer later supplies
the startup attitude in Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}.

The measurement-only choice is not merely a proof convenience.  Levelling with
the MEKF closes a gain loop from attitude error into the estimated period and
therefore back into the filter.  Using unlevelled body-$Z$ is exogenous but
fails spectrally: gravity leakage is heavily amplified by the integrations used
to infer elevation.  The two alternatives remain available as ablations; the
deployed proxy gives the useful combination of levelling and one-way
information flow required by the stability assumptions of
Sec.~\ref{app:iss-tuner}.

\subsection{Wave-period estimate}
\label{sec:wave-band-period}

The acceleration-band carrier used by the wave-direction demodulator is not the
tuning frequency.  Because acceleration weights an elevation spectrum by
$\omega^4$, that carrier remains near the upper energetic part of the spectrum
and changes little as the sea period grows.  The adaptation path instead
estimates the elevation zero-crossing period
$T_z=2\pi\sqrt{m_0/m_2}$.

The levelled acceleration $a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ is passed through two high-pass
stages and two leaky integrators with common corner $\lambda$, forming velocity
and elevation proxies $v_p$ and $\eta_p$.  Their common filtering cancels in
the ratio, giving for a narrow band
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sigma_{v_p}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}}=\sqrt{\omega^{2}+\lambda^{2}},
\qquad
\widehat{\omega}^{2}
=\frac{\sigma_{v_p}^{2}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}^{2}}-\lambda^{2},
\qquad
\widehat{T}_z=\frac{2\pi}{\widehat{\omega}} .
\label{eq:wave-band-period}
\end{equation}
The two high-pass stages keep very-low-frequency strapdown leakage from
controlling the double-integrated proxy while leaving the ratio in
\eqref{eq:wave-band-period} unchanged.  The estimator never reads the MEKF
position or velocity.

As soon as the wave-period estimator has a usable value, the tuner frequency is
$1/\widehat T_z$; before that, a bounded wave-band prior is used.  A fixed-horizon
EMA produces
\begin{equation}
f_{\mathrm{tune}}
=\clip\!\left(\overline{1/\widehat{T}_z},
 f_{\mathrm{tune,min}},f_{\mathrm{tune,max}}\right).
\label{eq:tuning-frequency}
\end{equation}
The deployed frequency-smoothing time constant is \SI{1}{s}.  This frequency
sets the OU time scale, the sigma-band corners, and the variance horizon.  The
separate acceleration carrier remains in the implementation for wave direction
only and is not used by the deployed OU-III tuning path.

\subsection{Acceleration-scale estimate and averaging}

The stationary OU acceleration scale is estimated from the same measurement-only
vertical channel.  Before its variance is formed, the signal passes through a
period-scaled wave band whose corners remain fixed multiples of
$f_{\mathrm{tune}}$.  The variance horizon follows a fixed number of estimated
periods,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
T_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\frac{1}
 {\clip(f_{\mathrm{tune}},f_{\mathrm{var,min}},f_{\mathrm{var,max}})},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{var}}&=\clip\!\left(K_{\mathrm{periods}}T_{\mathrm{eff}},
 \tau_{\mathrm{var,min}},\tau_{\mathrm{var,max}}\right),\\
\alpha_{\mathrm{var}}&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{var}}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sigma-variance-horizon}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses $K_{\mathrm{periods}}=2$ and clamps the horizon to
$[\SI{0.3}{s},\SI{60}{s}]$.  A two-stage EWMA at this horizon maintains the
band-limited second moment.  The corresponding band-adjusted sensor-noise floor
is subtracted before the wave component is square-rooted,
\begin{equation}
\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
=\max\!\left(0,
 \widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-\sigma_{\mathrm{nf,band}}^2\right).
\label{eq:sigma-noise-subtraction}
\end{equation}
Thus increasing sea period lengthens the statistical averaging interval rather
than changing the number of wave cycles represented by the estimate.

\subsection{Targets, smoothing, and application cadence}

The raw engineering targets are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau\frac{\widehat T_z}{2},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma
 \sqrt{\max(\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2,\epsilon)},\\
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{base}}&=c_R\sigma_{aw,\star}\tau_\star^3 .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuner-targets}
\end{equation}
The deployed coefficients are $(c_\tau,c_\sigma,c_R)=(1.0,0.9,0.35)$.  These
coefficients are empirical calibration constants.  The cubic base scale is
motivated by the similarity analysis of Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-motivation};
that analysis is not claimed to compute the complete-MEKF optimum.

Applied $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ use a common EMA,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\tau_\star-\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}),\\
\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\sigma_{aw,\star}-\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-ewma}
\end{equation}
with deployed $\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}=\SI{1.8}{s}$.  The regularizer has a
slower sea-scaled horizon,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_{r_S}&=m_{r_S}\tau_\star,\qquad
\alpha_{r_S}=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{r_S}},\\
r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}&\leftarrow
 r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}
 +\alpha_{r_S}(r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{base}}
              -r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-RS}
\end{equation}
where $m_{r_S}=5$.  Candidates are committed at \SI{0.1}{s} cadence and are
staged by one IMU sample: the MEKF processes $y_k$ with parameters measurable
with respect to data through $k-1$, and a candidate updated using $y_k$ first
becomes active at step $k+1$.

The pseudo-measurement cadence follows the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\qquad
c_T=\frac{\SI{15}{ms}}{\SI{1.1}{s}},
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}
\end{equation}
with $T_{S,\min}=\SI{5}{ms}$ and $T_{S,\max}=\SI{250}{ms}$.  To preserve the
historical information rate when this cadence changes, the standard deviation
actually passed to the Kalman update is
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}
 \sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}},
\qquad T_{S,0}=\SI{15}{ms}.
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter}
\end{equation}
Away from clamps, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:tuner-targets},
\eqref{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}, and
\eqref{eq:engineering-rs-filter} make the effective filter-input dependence
$r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}$.  This is the implemented
schedule evaluated in the reported experiments; the earlier cubic expression is
the base target before cadence renormalization.

\subsection{Three-axis application}

The current OU--III operating point is isotropic in the OU stationary scale,
\[
(\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}})^{1/2}
=\diag(\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\]
and in the integral pseudo-measurement standard deviation,
\begin{equation}
\vct r_S=
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
\end{bmatrix}^{\top},
\qquad
\mat R_S=\diag(\vct r_S\odot\vct r_S).
\label{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}
\end{equation}
The important empirical observation is not an axis-specific optimum.  The
current paired evidence shows that isotropic regularization works well over the
tested directional records.  Separate universal $x$ and $y$ tuning ratios are
not inferred from this finite set because surge and sway are projections of the
same directional wave motion and therefore depend strongly on heading.  The
larger horizontal drift source is addressed analytically in
Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits} and empirically in the 3-D results, not by
claiming a heading-independent horizontal multiplier.

